<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phan Bội Châu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Phan Boi Chau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phan Bội Châu was a Vietnamese nationalist and scholar who sought to free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> from French rule. He founded the Modernisation Society in 1904 and the Đông Du movement, which sent young Vietnamese to study in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and later formed a revolutionary league in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Seized by French agents in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in 1925, he spent his last years under house arrest in Huế.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phan-boi-chau.jpg" alt="Phan Bội Châu" width="225" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phan_Boi_Chau.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Phan was born Phan Văn San on 26 December 1867 in the village of Sa Nam, in Nam Đàn District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nghe-an-province">Nghệ An province</a>, to Phan Văn Phổ, of a poor family of scholars. His mother taught him passages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Classic of Poetry</a>, and from the age of five he studied the Chinese classics in his father's classes, memorising the Three Character Classic in three days. At 22 he married Nguyễn Thị Huy in an arranged match. He passed the regional examinations in 1900, not to become an official but to add weight to his anti-colonial work, and after his father died that year he turned to revolution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1903 he found a figurehead in Prince Cường Để, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Gia Long</a>'s eldest son, and wrote his first significant work, Letter from the Ryukyu written in Tears of Blood. Sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1905 to seek military aid, he found none, and on the advice of Liang Qichao turned to writing; his History of the Loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> of 1905 helped revive the name Vietnam. Through the Đông Du movement the number of Vietnamese students in Japan reached 200 in 1908, but after the Franco-Japanese Treaty of 1907 Japan expelled him under French pressure.</p><p>Inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang-uprising">Wuchang Uprising</a>, he and others founded the Vietnam Restoration League in late March 1912, with Cường Để as president and Phan as vice-president. Chinese authorities later held him in prison for almost four years, until 1917, where he wrote his own biography among other books. At the start of 1921 he studied socialism and met Soviet representatives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, though he later called class struggle in Vietnam extremely foolish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his seizure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in 1925 he was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> and convicted of treason. Anti-communist authors claimed Hồ Chí Minh had betrayed his arrival to the French for money; Sophie Quinn-Judge and Duncan McCargo argue that this is likely propaganda, since the French already had what they needed from their own spies. Phan died on 29 October 1940. Huỳnh Thúc Kháng led the building of his tomb and temple in Huế, a national relic since 1990, and his memorial site in Nam Đàn became a special national relic in 2016. Most Vietnamese cities have major streets named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phan Bội Châu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most important, colonial rule produced the movements that ended it: reformers such as <b>Phan Boi Chau</b> and Phan Chau Trinh, the Vietnamese Nationalist Party and above all the communist movement of Nguyen Ai Quoc (Ho Chi Minh), who founded the Vietnamese Communist Party in Hong Kong in February…</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nam Đàn, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in Huế, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.7,105.5&amp;year=1914" aria-label="Nam Đàn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Phan Bội Châu"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3838 5886h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3838 5886h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.7,105.5&amp;year=1914">What was here in 1914? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
